A: It seems like the test is asking for an Assert to verify the last positions of the item in the original and new ArrayLists are different. This would most likely cause an error if the item never existed in the list in the first place, since there would be no last index - hence it gets Asserted to be always -1.
You need to change this:
Assert(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX == New_arrListLastIndexOfX - 1, "Last Index of x in old list should be same as new list or its previous position.");

to:
Assert(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX != New_arrListLastIndexOfX, "Last Index of x in old list should be different than new list.");

This line will validate that the old and new positions of x are different which means that a new item was inserted. This will fail the assertion if the item is not supposed to be inserted (if you've provided the correct scenario) or if you're performing an operation outside of the method under test.  
Also, make sure your test inputs do not cause index out of bounds exceptions (e.g., when index is equal to arrList.Count). This is a point not dealt with in this question.
Lastly, make sure to thoroughly test edge cases (e.g., index = 0) as they may affect your testing results.

A: The issue arises because your ArrayList is empty when you insert. In that case, LastIndexOf returns -1. This implies that the index you inserted at is effectively the last item in the ArrayList. Hence, New_arrListLastIndexOfX will equals to Old_arrListCount which will cause the assertion to fail.
This happens because you're testing the behavior of Insert method, which can place x at the specified index in ArrayList arrList. The behavior you seem to be testing doesn't fit with the fact that Pex generates an ArrayList that is empty (since arrList.Count is initially 0), and your method under test has no requirements about the initial content of the list. That is not a typical way in which such test methods are used in a testing framework such as Pex.
If the Count of the ArrayList in Pex is initially more than 1, it's completely different story. It might be a problem with your inputs assumptions.
In order to test Insert method, initialize your ArrayList with some items before running Pex scenarios with Insert calls.
I hope this explanation clarifies something, and could help you on your way.
In any case, I strongly suggest you to rethink about your approach, considering other methods like Add, or maybe manually checking ArrayList after Pex scenario executes as it does not seem an appropriate way for automated testing with tools like Pex.
Regards,

A: Here's how I'd write the contract:


*

*Assuming a precondition such that index is between 0 and arrList.Count, inclusive.

*Storing old values: Count, Contains(x), IndexOf(x), and the value at index.  

*Invoking the PUT method, which should not throw and return the inserted item.

*Assert on the postconditions. These would include:


*

*arrList.Count is incremented by one.

*arrList.Contains(x) returns true.

*arrList.IndexOf(x) > old IndexOf(x) if x was not in the list.

*arrList[index] gives you the inserted item x.



So the contract would look like:
[PexMethodUnderTest]
[PexExpectedException(typeof(ArgumentException))]  // Thrown when index is out of range
public void PUT_InsertContract([PexAssumeUnderTest] ArrayList arrList, int x, int index)
{
   Assume.True(index >= 0 && index <= arrList.Count);
   int oldCount = arrList.Count;
   bool oldContains = arrList.Contains(x);
   int oldIndexOf = arrList.IndexOf(x);
   int oldItem;
   if (index < arrList.Count)
     oldItem = (int)arrList[index];
   else
     oldItem = 0; // whatever default value you want to have--------------------	    int Old_index_of_x_in_list = Old_arrListIndexOfX(Old_x) ; 
	    int New_index_of_x_in_list = New_arrListIndexOfX(New_x) ; 

	    if (Old_x_last_position==New_x_last_position) 
	    {
		    Debug.Assert("The last position of 'x' in the old and new list is the same"==false);
	    }

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that @@@ the count of the old list increased by one
	    Debug.Assert(Old_arrList_Count + 1 == New_arrList_Count);

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ the index of 'x' in new list is greater than its index in old list
	    Debug.Assert(Old_arrList_IndexOf_x + 1 == New_arrList_IndexOf_x);

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that @@@ the index of 'x' in new list and last index of 'x' in new list is the same 
	    Debug.Assert(New_arrList_IndexOf_x == New_arrList_LastIndexOf_x );

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that @@@ the new list contains 'x' 
	    Debug.Assert(New_arrList_contains_x==true);
        }
    }
}

