A: In your contract test, you'd have to reconsider the way you verify your array list modifications. The PexAssert statements are only used during the dynamic part of the test (the actual behavior under test) and are thus of use only for simple checks and don't fit in the context of contract tests (those aim to make sure the public contract always remains the same). 

Moreover, when you are writing an ArrayList test using Pex – the goal is not to test its behavior but to test your whole program behaviour. It should be based on the behavior of your application, rather than the behavior of something that is tested automatically. 

In other words, you should only be verifying behavior after all other functionality of your program is verified, not while in the middle of testing the behavior of a particular method. 

So the code block for verifying your array list modifications should go at the end of your testing function, after you've verified the entire program's behavior, not during the dynamic part of test (during PexAssert usage).

Here is an example of how your test could be written:

    [TestMethod]
    [PexMethod]
    [HostType("Msil")]
    public void ArrayListInsertContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x, int index)
    {
        PUT_InsertContract_DynamicPart(arrList, x, index);

        // Now that we have ensured that the dynamic part of our test is working, 
        // we have to verify things in the context of the whole program's behavior.
        // For instance, you would have checked the behavior of your application 
        // in relation to the ArrayList, not just the insert method:

        if (index >= 0 && index < arrList.Count)
        {
            int expectedCount = arrList.Count;
            int actualCount = arrList.Count;
            Assert.AreEqual(expectedCount, actualCount, "The count of the list should not have changed");

            int expectedIndex = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            int actualIndex = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            Assert.AreEqual(expectedIndex, actualIndex, "The index of the inserted item should be correct");
        }
        else
        {
            // Check other behaviors of your application that this insertion is not making
        }
    } 

Lastly, remember that Pex aims to test the public contracts of your public classes and methods, not the internal behavior. Thus, it's good practice to ignore or isolate any internal methods and properties when writing contract tests using Pex. If your test coverage includes such methods/properties, consider disabling them for the sake of Pex testing – do remember to re-enable them in your production code. – user.

Source: http://research.microsoft.com/en-us/um/cambridge/proj/pex/

