### Example 4: Postcondition [#postcondition](https://osherove.com/tdd-kata-2#postcondition)

```csharp
namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        public void POST_CountContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList)
        {
            int oldCount = arrList.Count;

            // Assume we have added a new element to arrList
            arrList.Add(1); 

            int newCount = arrList.Count;

            // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  newCount == oldCount + 1
            Assert.AreEqual(oldCount + 1, newCount);
        }
    }
}
```

## Assertions and Debug Statements
Assertions and `Debug.WriteLine` statements help keep track of the state of the solution during testing. Assertions will halt test execution if the condition specified in their arguments is not met. Debug.WriteLine statements can be used to print out important information during testing.

### Example 5: Assertion [#assertion](https://osherove.com/tdd-kata-2#assertion)

```csharp
namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    [TestClass]
    public class ArrayListTest
    {
        [TestMethod]
        public void TestAddMethod()
        {
            ArrayList arrList = new ArrayList();
            arrList.Add("Hello");

            // Assertion used to test if the addition of element was successful
            Assert.AreEqual(1, arrList.Count);
        }
    }
}
```
In this example, we are testing the `Add` method. By adding an element to `arrList` before the test, and then checking the `Count` property for `arrList`, we can ensure that the addition was successful. If the count does not equal 1, the test will fail which confirms the correctness of the `Add` method.

### Example 6: Debug Statement [#debugging](https://osherove.com/tdd-kata-2#debugging)

```csharp
namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    [TestClass]
    public class ArrayListTest
    {
        [TestMethod]
        public void TestIndexerProperty()
        {
            ArrayList arrList = new ArrayList();
            arrList.Add("Hello");
            arrList.Add("World");

            // Printing the value at the 0th index of the arrayList to console
            Debug.WriteLine(arrList[0]);
            // Printing the value at the 1st index of the arrayList to console
            Debug.WriteLine(arrList[1]);

            // Assertion used to test if the indexer property is working correctly
            Assert.AreEqual("Hello", arrList[0]);
            Assert.AreEqual("World", arrList[1]);
        }
    }
}
```
In this example, we are testing the `Indexer Property`. By adding elements to `arrList` and then checking the values they contain with the indexer property, we can ensure that this property works correctly. The Debug.WriteLine can be used to print out the values to the console, which is useful for inspecting the data at various points in the test.

## Conclusion
Creating test-driven tests using unit tests, contracts and assertions allows you to verify the behavior of your code as you write or refactor it. It not only makes your tests more specific and faster, but it also provides an easy way to check that your code is working correctly in its current state.

Please make sure to use the proper tools and frameworks for your testing needs. F# is a functional programming language that also provides strong support for unit testing and can be combined with NUnit, xUnit or MSpec, for example. On the other hand, TDD, BDD and pair programming are not exclusive to C# and have proven to be effective for many other languages as well.
--------------------